{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2140-11-18 12:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, I’m Dr. Lee, one of your transplant surgeons. We’re about to take you to surgery for your kidney transplant today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2140-11-18 12:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I’ve been waiting a long time for this. Is everything ready?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2140-11-18 12:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, everything’s set. We’ll be using a deceased donor pediatric en bloc transplant, which means two small kidneys will be placed together. We expect good function, but there may be some challenges due to your prior surgeries."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2140-11-18 12:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember last time I was in for that cough, they were worried about infection. Is that still a concern?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2140-11-18 12:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good question. Your recent illness was viral, not bacterial, and your blood cultures were negative. We stopped antibiotics before this admission. We’ll monitor closely, but you’re cleared for surgery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2140-11-19 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. The transplant surgery went through, but it was complex. There was a lot of scar tissue, and we had to transfuse platelets due to oozing during the procedure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2140-11-19 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, that sounds rough. Are the kidneys working?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2140-11-19 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Urine output is low right now, which we call slow graft function. It’s common, especially with pediatric kidneys. We’re giving IV fluids and Lasix to help. Your creatinine was 8.4 on admission—we’ll watch for it to come down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2140-11-19 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the liver biopsy you mentioned? Is that serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2140-11-19 09:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The liver looked firm and nodular, so we took a biopsy. Results aren’t back yet, but we’re checking for hepatitis and other issues. We’ll get Hepatology involved if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2140-11-20 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your renal ultrasound shows both kidneys have good blood flow. No blockage or leaks. That’s excellent news."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2140-11-20 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. My belly feels swollen though."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2140-11-20 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we saw some ascites—fluid in the abdomen. It could be from heart or liver issues. We’ll monitor it. Your JP drain is collecting fluid, and we tested it—no leak from the kidneys."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2140-11-21 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "HBV testing came back: you’re positive for core and surface antibodies, meaning past exposure. No active virus detected. But we’ll start Lamivudine to prevent reactivation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2140-11-21 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that affect my transplant?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2140-11-21 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, it’s preventive. Hepatitis B can reactivate under immunosuppression. This protects your liver and transplant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2140-11-22 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your creatinine dropped to 2.0, and urine output is improving. That’s a great sign your kidneys are waking up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2140-11-22 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. But I’ve been short of breath. Is that related?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2140-11-22 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. You’re showing signs of volume overload. Cardiology is coming to evaluate. Your heart function has been reduced, and the extra fluid may be stressing it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2140-11-23 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The echo shows your heart is working at 40-45% and you have moderate pulmonary hypertension. Atrial tachycardia is likely contributing. We’ll increase Lasix to help remove fluid."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2140-11-23 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need a rhythm procedure?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2140-11-23 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly, but not now. Anticoagulation would be needed, and it’s too risky with your recent surgery. You’ll follow up with electrophysiology as an outpatient."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2140-11-24 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Liver biopsy results are back: stage IV fibrosis—severe scarring. Iron is building up in liver cells. This suggests long-term damage, possibly from metabolic or toxic causes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2140-11-24 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that cirrhosis?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2140-11-24 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, essentially. We’ll keep you on Lamivudine and monitor. You’ll need an EGD to check for varices—swollen veins in the stomach—because of the portal hypertension."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2140-11-26 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The EGD was attempted yesterday, but your stomach wasn’t empty. We repeated it today and found mild portal gastropathy and a small nodule. Biopsy showed no cancer or serious changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2140-11-26 11:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2140-11-26 11:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not at this time. We’ll keep you on omeprazole and monitor. The main concern was bleeding risk from varices, which we didn’t see."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2140-11-27 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your blood sugars have been high. The steroids are making it worse. We tried glipizide, but now we’re switching to insulin—Lantus at night and sliding scale during the day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2140-11-27 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I was on oral meds before. Will I need insulin forever?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2140-11-27 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not necessarily. Once the steroids are tapered, we may adjust. But with diabetes and transplant, blood sugar control is critical to healing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2140-11-28 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your white count dropped, so we reduced CellCept. It can suppress bone marrow. We’re now at 500 mg twice daily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2140-11-28 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I had diarrhea too. Is that related?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2140-11-28 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, CellCept can cause that. The dose reduction should help. C. diff test was negative, so it’s likely medication-related."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2140-11-29 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did a paracentesis today—removed 240 cc of fluid. The cell count suggests peritonitis. We’re starting fluconazole while we wait for culture results."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2140-11-29 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Peritonitis? Is that serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2140-11-29 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can be, but we caught it early. The culture grew Candida krusei, a yeast. We’ll switch to micafungin, which works better against this type."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2140-11-30 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your creatinine is now 1.2—excellent! The JP drain is out, but we’re keeping Lasix at 40 mg to prevent more fluid buildup."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2140-11-30 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. But I feel a leak near my incision."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2140-11-30 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, ascites is leaking from the right lower incision. We’ll increase Lasix to 60 mg and monitor. If it continues, we may need to open part of the wound."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2140-12-01 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The ultrasound shows the fluid collection is still there. Resistive indices are a bit high in one kidney, but we’re not sure if it’s technical or real. We’ll repeat soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2140-12-01 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2140-12-01 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not yet. It could just be how the machine read it. Your urine output and creatinine are stable, which is most important."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2140-12-02 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The leak from your incision is worse. We’ll do another paracentesis to remove fluid and reduce pressure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2140-12-03 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We removed 310 cc of fluid yesterday. But the incision is still draining. We’ve decided to partially open it and place a wound VAC to help it heal from the inside out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2140-12-03 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A vacuum? That sounds intense."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2140-12-03 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s very effective. It reduces fluid buildup and helps new tissue grow. A visiting nurse will change the dressing three times a week at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2140-12-04 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The VAC is working—output dropped from 380 cc to much less. We’re lowering Lasix back to 20 mg for a few days to balance fluid removal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2140-12-05 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re placing a PICC line in your left arm so you can get IV micafungin at home for two weeks. Your dialysis access is in both arms, so we’re using the brachial vein."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2140-12-05 11:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that interfere with my dialysis?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2140-12-05 11:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, the PICC is for antibiotics only and won’t affect your fistula. It’ll be removed after treatment."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2140-12-06 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Urine culture showed yeast, but it’s likely from the treatment. We’re continuing micafungin. Blood cultures are negative—no systemic infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2140-12-07 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. The VAC output is minimal, and you’re walking independently. Home health will assist with wound care and meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2140-12-07 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "When can I go home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2140-12-07 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "If all goes well, discharge is planned for tomorrow. You’ll need to monitor weight, blood sugar, and blood pressure daily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2140-12-08 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Final plan: You’re on tacrolimus, CellCept, and prednisone—we’ve stopped steroids due to blood sugar issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2140-12-08 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the insulin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2140-12-08 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, Lantus at night and sliding scale. Check sugars four times a day. Call the clinic if over 200 consistently."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2140-12-08 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the heart and liver?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2140-12-08 08:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue carvedilol and amlodipine. Follow up with cardiology and hepatology. The cirrhosis needs monitoring, but your transplant is stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2140-12-08 08:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the wound?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2140-12-08 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The VAC stays on. Nurse changes it three times a week. Watch for redness, fever, or increased drainage—call the clinic immediately if any."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2140-12-08 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you for everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2140-12-08 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve worked hard. Keep up the good care, and we’ll see you in the clinic next week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2140-12-09 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "All set for discharge. Your condition is stable, mental status clear, and you’re walking well. Home health is scheduled."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2140-12-09 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m ready to go home. It’s been a long stay."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2140-12-09 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It has. But your kidneys are working, the infection is under control, and the wound is healing. Keep following the instructions closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 68,
      "time": "2140-12-09 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you, Doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 69,
      "time": "2140-12-09 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Call the transplant clinic with any concerns. Take care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 70,
      "time": "2140-12-09 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. See you soon."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2140-11-18 11:42:00",
    "admission_end": "2140-12-09 15:15:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A male patient with end-stage renal disease, diabetes, and heart failure was admitted for a deceased donor pediatric en bloc kidney transplant. The surgery was complicated by significant scarring, intraoperative oozing requiring platelet transfusion, and the incidental finding of a nodular liver leading to a biopsy that revealed stage IV fibrosis. Postoperatively, he developed slow graft function, which resolved as creatinine improved from 8.4 to 1.2 and urine output increased. He experienced volume overload and atrial tachycardia, managed with diuresis and rate control, with plans for outpatient rhythm evaluation. Ascites developed, and a paracentesis revealed Candida krusei peritonitis, treated with micafungin via a newly placed PICC line. A surgical wound leak necessitated placement of a wound VAC. Hyperglycemia from steroid use required insulin initiation. He was discharged home with home health support, on immunosuppression, antifungal therapy, and wound care, with stable graft function and resolved complications.",
    "problems": [
      "End stage renal disease",
      "Diabetes with renal manifestations, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Alcoholic cirrhosis of liver",
      "Portal hypertension",
      "Other suppurative peritonitis",
      "Candidiasis of other urogenital sites",
      "Complications of transplanted kidney",
      "Disruption of external operation (surgical) wound",
      "Acute on chronic diastolic heart failure",
      "Atrial flutter",
      "Hemorrhage complicating a procedure",
      "Surgical operation with transplant of whole organ causing abnormal patient reaction, or later complication, without mention of misadventure at time of operation",
      "Diarrhea",
      "Hypercalcemia",
      "Congestive heart failure, unspecified",
      "Polyneuropathy in diabetes",
